the E. Wizard@har 
> I liked best about Moria was that even superpowerful characters are
> still vulnerable, cause there's no resistance to poison gas.  In nethack
> high level characters are closer to Sherman Tanks than adventurers.  
> Knowing you can only be killed by getting surrounded by _huge_ mobs 
> makes individual monsters really useless.  "Oh, look, Demogorgon. <yawn>"

This invulnerability becomes quite pronounced, as anybody who has
decided to start killing things with their bare hands has discovered. 
Even being surrounded by monsters rarely works, since your average 30th
level adventurer has not only several amulets of life saving, but will
also be able to teleport away, polymorph, blast away with wands and just
generally sneer at anything short of 100 Archons.  Matter o' fact,
Archons are the only thing that can even vaugely come close to a high
level adventurer.  Something must be done.  The nethack difficulty curve
has got to be flattened out some.  Do I have a suggestion?  Not really. 
I think that one illogical facet of the game is that monsters won't use
wands of cancellation on you.  That, right there, would make the latter
part of the game more difficult, since you'd have +0 armor for the most
part and life's alot tougher without that -40 AC.  I'd also like to see
more ways for armor/weapons/stuff to get stolen or destroyed.  How about
something like a gremlin (steals intrinsics) that's alot tougher (and
alot better at stealing intrinsics)?  My Crash and Burn of the Valkyrie
was precipitated in fairly large part by stuff getting stolen along with
intrinsics.  Just a couple of suggestions.

